parts.text:
The fixture picks seed=0 with from=province_16 (3 armies) to=province_2 (3 armies). After one exchange, attacker loses 1 (rolled 1 vs 6). So province_16 has 2 armies, threshold=1, so 2 > 1, continues running. data-running=true. ✓

But what if it's a different seed where attacker doesn't lose 1 on first exchange? The fixture doesn't constrain on the outcome, so the loop iterates over all seeds until it finds one where the preparation succeeds.

Actually wait - re-reading the fixture, with kind="manual", the FIRST seed where the legal attack can be set up (sulo with all income placed, then endReinforcement), is selected. The first outcome is whatever it happens to be.

Since attack probabilities are roughly even (50/50-ish for 1-die combat), roughly half of all seeds will have source = 3 + outcome = defender wins (source unchanged). Half will have source drop to 2. Either way, with threshold=1, both scenarios give running=true. ✓

If source had 2 armies (impossible since with placement of all income on 1 province, that province gets +income; let me check), wait `start.reinforcementPool` is the player's starting pool. They place all of it on from, so from = from's starting armies + pool. Pool is small in initial deal, maybe 5-10. So source should be at least 5+.

OK so running=true is reliable.

Now the test "fight-on stops at the chosen fixed threshold after a loss":
```
test("fight-on stops at the chosen fixed threshold after a loss",async({page},info)=>{
  const errors:string[]=[];page.on("pageerror",e=>errors.push(e.message));
  const f=await prepare(page,"loss"),threshold=f.ready.armies[f.from]!-1;
  await page.locator("#battle-stop-threshold").fill(String(threshold));await page.locator("#battle-stop-threshold").press("Tab");
  await page.locator("#fight-on-button").click();
  const next=step(f.ready,{kind:"attack",from:f.from,to:f.to});
  expect(next.armies[f.from]).toBe(threshold);  // <-- source dropped to threshold
  await expect(region(page)).toHaveAttribute("data-running","false");  // <-- battle stopped
  await expect(page.locator("#battle-stop-threshold")).toHaveValue(String(threshold));
  await page.clock.runFor(4000);
  await history(page,next.history.length);await paint(page,next);await context(page,next,f.from,f.to);
  await capture(page,"threshold-stop",info.project.name);
  expect(errors).toEqual([]);
});
```

For "loss", the fixture constrains that the first outcome has `attackerLosses=1`. So source starts at N, loses 1 to N-1. The threshold is set to N-1. After one exchange, source = N-1 <= threshold → running=false.

Then `clock.runFor(4000)` - this advances virtual time by 4 seconds. But the battle is stopped (data-running=false). No scheduled exchange. History unchanged.

`history(page, next.history.length)` confirms no history change. Good.

But wait, is this what's tested in the controller? Let me re-verify:
```
const threshold = armyCount(result.state, from) <= this.current.effectiveThreshold;
```

After exchange, if `armyCount(result.state, from) <= effectiveThreshold`, running = false. With source=N, exchange reduces by 1 to N-1. effectiveThreshold = N-1. So N-1 <= N-1 → true → running=false. ✓

This is correct.

OK let me also look at one more potential issue - what if the game wins mid-battle?

Looking at combat.ts:
```
if (armyCount(state, toId) <= 0) {
  // Capture: ...
  if (rules.capture === "choice") {
    ...
    state.pendingCapture = ...
    state.phase = "capture";
  }
}
```

If rules.combat.capture === "choice", the phase is "capture". 

Looking at rules.ts (endReinforcement + endTurn):
```
function endTurn(state: GameState): ApplyResult {
  ...
  if (state.rules.combat.capture === "choice" && checkVictory(state)) return { ok: true, state, outcome: null };
  ...
}
```

So when endTurn is called and capture === "choice" and checkVictory (only one living), the game ends with state.phase = "finished".

So in our flow:
- capture happens, phase = "capture"
- user confirms captureMove
- state.phase = "attack" (since state.pendingCapture is cleared)
- user calls endTurn
- apply: endTurn → checkVictory → state.phase = "finished"

The controller's dispatch(action):
```
const result = apply(this.state, action);
if (!result.ok) return false;
this.cancel();
this.publish({ state: result.state, from: null, to: null, effectiveThreshold: 1,
  lastOutcome: null, rolls: [], ties: 0, attackerWins: 0, defenderWins: 0, message: "",
});
```

After dispatch, state is updated to the result (finished). Good.

OK now let me also re-check the "capture overflow" mention in the README and the "capture minimum/maximum" test for edge cases.

Looking at the capture `confirm-capture-button` click test:
```
await page.locator("#capture-amount").fill("0");
await page.locator("#confirm-capture-button").dispatchEvent("click");
await history(page, next.history.length);  // still in capture
```

After fill 0 and dispatch click, confirmCapture(0, revision) is called. amount=0 < pending.min=1, so apply returns ok:false. confirmCapture returns false. `amount.reportValidity()` is called - browser would show validation but no state change.

Then:
```
await page.locator("#capture-minimum-button").click();  // sets amount to 1
await page.locator("#capture-more-button").click();  // sets amount to 2
await page.locator("#capture-less-button").click();  // sets amount to 1
```

These use the `choose` function:
```
const choose = (value: number) => { amount.value = String(Math.max(pending.min, Math.min(pending.max, value))); };
```

So `choose(pending.min + 1)` clamps to pending.max (whatever that is). The test expects "2" for #capture-more-button click after min. So pending.max >= 2. Good.

`choose(pending.min + 1 - 1)` = `choose(pending.min)` = pending.min. So value is 1.

Now `await page.locator("#confirm-capture-button").click()` calls confirmCapture(amount.valueAsNumber, revision). amount.value is "1" (or max).

After confirmCapture, the test:
```
await oldConfirm!.evaluate(n => n.dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent("click", { bubbles: true })));
```

oldConfirm is the now-detached button. Its click listener calls `confirmCapture(amount.valueAsNumber, oldRevision)`. With oldRevision !== current, accepts returns false, confirmCapture returns false. amount.reportValidity() is called (but amount is still valid).

State history doesn't change.

Then `clock.runFor(2000)` and assert history unchanged.

OK all these guard paths are intact.

Let me also check if there's a subtle issue with the generated game:

